  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying the outcome with the minimum probability in each lottery and comparing their favorability. However, the individual does not focus on the least probable outcomes in this way. Lottery A’s least probable outcome is -3000 at 0.2%, and Lottery B’s is -6000 at 0.1%—so the minimum probability events differ in both probability and outcome. The decision reason would favor Lottery A because its least probable outcome (-3000) is more favorable than Lottery B’s (-6000), but the individual chose Lottery B. More importantly, their justification is based on the lower probability of loss (0.1% vs 0.2%), not on a comparison of the least probable outcomes as defined by the reason. They appear to treat the loss outcomes as the primary events to evaluate and prefer a lower chance of any loss, which aligns more with a probability-minimizing heuristic than with the specific logic of comparing least probable outcomes. There is no evidence that they identified or compared the outcomes with minimum probability in the way the reason prescribes. Therefore, the decision reason was not used.",
